    System.out.println("str1 and str2 have the same value");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 have different values");
}
```

In the above example, `str1` and `str2` are two different string objects with the same value, but using the `==` operator will return `false`, indicating that they refer to different objects. However, using the `equals()` method will return `true`, indicating that they have the same value.

Therefore, to compare strings in Java, always use the `equals()` method instead of the `==` operator. You can also override the `equals()` method in your custom classes if you want to provide a custom comparison logic.

